`appendHiddenRange` appends ONE record — atomic. Good.

Now the remaining critical question — the Flutter app's `_relieveOverWindow` change. Compare to the CLI:

CLI `_relieveOverWindow`:
```dart
_pushBusyPhase('Compacting context…');
final reliefOutcome = await _runEmergencyRelief();
_pushBusyPhase('');
final after = _agent.state.messages.toList();
final afterTokens = _liveRequestTokens();
if (afterTokens >= beforeTokens) { ...no-op; return null; }
... print '[resuming] ...' if under window
return after;
``

Flutter `_relieveOverWindow`:
```dart
// persist drain (pre-existing)
final wiring = _compactionWiring;
if (wiring.conversationWindow > 0) {
 await EmergencyRelief(...).run();
}
final after = _agent.state.messages.toList();
final afterTokens = estimateRequestTokens(after, ...);
if (afterTokens >= beforeTokens) { no-op log; return null; }
_persistedCount = after.length;
messages..clear()..addAll(after.map(_toChatMessage));
_notify();
...
return after;
``

Concern A: `_persistedCount = after.length` when the trim valve fired. As analyzed: state.messages is then the trimmed list (length L < projection P). Cursor = L. Since all projection records were persisted BEFORE the relief (drained), and the trimmed list is a subset-ish of persisted content, the cursor is used as `all.skip(_persistedCount)` over `all = state.messages` — the NEW list. Next persist: skip(L) over length-L list = nothing. New messages append at the right place. So NO duplication happens in the app's persist path, because the persist cursor indexes the in-memory list, and the dropped records are already on disk. The only anomaly: the session file has records that the live context dropped — by design ("in-memory only").

BUT — the subtle issue: on the NEXT relief/compaction, `refreshAgentState` re-anchors from the projection → the dropped records COME BACK (resurrect). That's true for the quality tier too (pre-existing valve semantics — same in the CLI funnel path at line 1152: `_agent.state.messages = trimmed.messages; _persistedCount = ...`). So resurrection is an accepted pre-existing tradeoff, presumably handled because the next compaction re-relieves. Not introduced by this PR. Not a finding — or at most digest-level.

Concern B: In the Flutter path, unlike the CLI, the outcome is discarded (`await ....run()` without keeping the outcome) — the diagnostic log doesn't include hidden/trim counts. Cosmetic only.

Concern C: Flutter `_relieveOverWindow` measures `beforeTokens` from `overWindow` (the guard's transcript) but the relief re-anchors state — if projection ≠ overWindow (e.g., in-flight messages), the comparison `afterTokens >= beforeTokens` mixes bases. Pre-existing shape (the old code called `_maybeAutoCompact()` and compared the same way). Not new.

Concern D — actually let me look more carefully at a REAL difference between CLI and Flutter paths: the CLI passes `window: _effectiveContextWindow` and `settings: _effectiveCompactionSettings`. The Flutter passes `wiring.conversationWindow` and `wiring.settings`. Need to confirm `_compactionWiring` is the same wiring used by `_maybeAutoCompact` (consistency). Let me check `_compactionWiring`.

Also `EmergencyRelief` in Flutter does NOT run when `conversationWindow <= 0` (guarded). In the CLI, `_effectiveContextWindow` — always > 0? The engine handles `window <= 0` by no-op anyway. Fine.

Now the one remaining significant-behavior question — **the relief's busy phase in the CLI**: old code wrapped `_runAutoCompact` in try/finally to always clear the busy phase; new code calls `_pushBusyPhase('Compacting context…'); final reliefOutcome = await _runEmergencyRelief(); _pushBusyPhase(');` — if `_runEmergencyRelief` THROWS, the busy phase is never cleared! The old code had `try { ... } finally { _pushBusyPhase('); }`. The new code drops the try/finally. Can `relief.run()` throw? The docstring says "never throws for a healthy session (a broken projection degrades to the trim valve; a failed hide append leaves the transcript untouched)". But `refreshAgentState` → `session.buildContextMessages()` can throw on a CORUPT session file (JSON decode error, torn write). `classicTransform(await session.getBranch())` can throw on repo errors. `_buildView` — `RecordSeqIndex(await session.getEntries())` — I/O errors. The catch in run() only wraps `appendHiddenRange`. So a broken session → run() throws → `_pushBusyPhase(')` never runs → the TUI busy label stays "Compacting context…" forever (until next phase change?). Who catches the throw? In the loop: `try { final relieved = await config.overWindowRelief!(context.messages); ... } catch (_) { ... }` — the loop catches (line ~1624: `} catch (_) {` — need to see what it does; probably treats relief failure as no relief → guard error). So the throw IS caught by the loop, the run continues to the guard error — but the CLI's busy phase stays stuck at 'Compacting context…'.

How does `_pushBusyPhase` work? Let me check. In the old code the finally was deliberate ("the relief path never showed the busy label — a 15-30 min compaction read as a dead, silent stall"). Both CLI call sites (`_relieveOverWindow`, `_relieveMessageCap`) now lack the try/finally. This is a regression vs the immediately preceding code — a deterministic-tier run makes throwing less likely (no LM), but session I/O can still throw. The stuck busy phase is exactly the "dead, silent stall" face issue #1085 M3 fought. Severity: this is a real-but-edge robustness regression — ⚠️ IMPORTANT? Let me verify `_pushBusyPhase` semantics first: maybe '' pushes are coalesced and the label is set per-step anyway (every step pushes its own label, so a stale label gets replaced on the next phase change). If the run continues (guard error path), the next phase push would overwrite. But if the relief throws and the loop surfaces the terminal guard error, the run ENDS — is the busy phase cleared at run end? Let me check `_pushBusyPhase` and the run teardown.

